{
  "requirement": "The requirements index MUST show each requirement's evidence state as Valid, Missing, Determination false, FIXME, or Invalid. Valid MUST mean its determination is true and its record passes the same evidence and reference coverage checks as `habeas::validate()`. A false or FIXME determination MUST have its distinct state when the record passes all other checks. The FIXME state MUST pass validation while retaining its distinct label. Malformed or stale evidence MUST remain Invalid regardless of its determination. Each detail page MUST show any validation findings.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment.** The implementation meets every obligation of `requirements.index.state`. The tests cover the interesting points. I found no unmarked implementation code and no unspecified behavior in the marked code.\n\n**Implementation (`src/index.rs`)**\n- `EvidenceState` has exactly the five states: Valid, Missing, DeterminationFalse, Fixme and Invalid. `EvidenceState::label` produces the required labels \"Valid\", \"Missing\", \"Determination false\", \"FIXME\" and \"Invalid\".\n- `LoadedDetailEvidence::load_path` derives the state from `inspect_repository`'s `EvidenceReport`, which is the same report `habeas::validate_repository` and `habeas::validate()` use. It keeps the report errors that start with the record's `evidence/<name>.json:` prefix.\n  - A record that is absent from disk is `Missing`.\n  - With no record errors, determination `true` is `Valid`, `false` is `DeterminationFalse`, and `FIXME` is `Fixme`. An unparseable judgment is `Invalid`.\n  - With errors, the record is `DeterminationFalse` only if every error is the \"determination is false\" finding. Otherwise it is `Invalid`. So stale text or inputs, malformed fields, a stale reference set, and uncovered `{impl}` or `{test}` references all give `Invalid`, whatever the determination.\n  - `FIXME` adds no validation error. `Judgment::from_value` accepts it, and `validate_evidence_file` only flags a false determination. So a clean FIXME record passes validation and keeps its own label, as the requirement says.\n- I traced the error prefixes in `src/evidence.rs`. Evidence-file checks, reference-set changes and \"no input covers\" errors all start with `evidence/<name>.json:`. They therefore reach the per-record state.\n- `DetailPlan::populate` writes each state label into the index rows. `render_detail_sections` shows the state badge and a \"Validation findings\" section listing all errors for the record, including for false and FIXME records.\n- All the pieces carry `{impl}` markers: the enum, `load`, `load_path`, `state`, `errors`, `label`, `color`, `populate` and `render_detail_sections`. The other users, `commands.rs` and `authoring/batch.rs`, only consume the state.\n\n**Tests**\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` checks Valid, Determination false, Invalid for changed text, Missing, and Invalid for an uncovered `{impl}`, in the index rows.\n- `findings_make_false_and_fixme_records_invalid` covers the combinations:\n  - false clean is Determination false;\n  - false with stale text, malformed fields, or an uncovered reference is Invalid;\n  - FIXME clean is FIXME;\n  - FIXME with stale text is Invalid.\n  It also asserts that the detail page shows \"Validation findings\" exactly when the state is not FIXME.\n- `tests/outcomes.rs` covers three more cases:\n  - a malformed FIXME is Invalid;\n  - a FIXME with a stale input or an uncovered reference is Invalid, and a valid FIXME stays visible in the index and detail page;\n  - a clean FIXME validates with no errors (`fixme_requirements_accept_issue_references_and_other_content`, which is tagged for `requirements.ci.determination`, not this requirement).\n\nThe tests do not exercise an unreadable (non-NotFound) evidence file, which is a trivial path that yields Invalid. There are no FIXME comments or other acknowledged gaps.",
  "references": "32af10e9f1acc3238e0ee16a28d200757007609134ecfd760adcd60ed0eb7e93",
